\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Corner and edge stabilizers of the cube}

\p{Let #{G} be the actual permutation group generated by the six
outer-face quarter-turns of the ordinary cube, with its frame of
reference fixed. Use the [corner and edge fibers](frap-000V): cyclic
corner labels with local group #{C_3}, and edge labels with local
group #{C_2}. This specializes the local-orientation construction of
\citet{Section 3.1}{marchetti2025rubik}; the orbit argument below
identifies the stabilizers needed for [transfer](frap-000X).}

\p{The action on eight corner positions is transitive. Adjacent
corners share a face whose quarter-turn takes one to the other, and
the cube's edge graph is connected. Hence a corner stabilizer
#{H_c} has index eight. Its action on the three cyclically ordered
labels gives a homomorphism #{\rho_c:H_c\to C_3}.
The action on twelve edge positions is also transitive: the four
edges of one face form a single orbit under its quarter-turn;
neighboring faces share an edge; and the face-adjacency graph is
connected. Thus an edge stabilizer #{H_e} has index twelve, with
local action #{\rho_e:H_e\to C_2}.}

\p{Choose a frame at the fixed corner or edge and transport it using
coset representatives. The transfer comparison gives}
##{\chi_c=(\rho_c)_{\mathrm{ab}}\circ T_{G,H_c},\qquad
   \chi_e=(\rho_e)_{\mathrm{ab}}\circ T_{G,H_e}.}
\p{We identify the abelianizations of #{C_3} and #{C_2} with those
groups. The [face-turn calculation](frap-000V) proves that the twist
and flip characters vanish on every legal generator, hence on #{G}.
It follows that these two local images of transfer vanish. It does
not follow that #{T_{G,H_c}} or #{T_{G,H_e}} is trivial in the larger
stabilizer abelianizations. No classification of #{G} was needed.}
